"EmbeddingGemma 2 is a best-in-class open model for natively multimodal embeddings (https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/embeddinggemma-2/)\nciteturn41353view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/embeddinggemma-2/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 299\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nORCA: The Annealed Spectral ConditioningOptimizer for Faster, Better LLM Training (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.06116v1)\nciteturn41353view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.06116v1\",\"lineno\":115}); Total lines: 498\nL69: (3) We demonstrate ORCA’s effectiveness across diverse LLM architectures and scales, achieving faster convergence and lower loss than existing optimizers. Through extensive controlled experiments, we further validate the mechanism that early effective-rank shaping drives the observed improvements.\nL70: ## 2 Spectral Structure and Optimization\nL71: \nL72: As noted in Section 1, Muon’s higher effective rank coincides with faster optimization. This observation suggests that properties of the weight spectrum may be useful for optimization, but whether they provide a general benefit remains unclear. To turn this still-vague possibility into a precise question about optimization dynamics, we ask:\nL73: \nL74: Q1. Can the spectral structure observed under Muon directly affect optimization speed, and through what mechanism?\nL75: A direct mechanism already appears in ’the multiplicatively coupled weight matrices’ of Transformers. Consider for example the value–output pair $\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ of a single attention head:\nL76: \nL77:  | $$\\operatorname{Attn}(\\mathbf{H})=\\operatorname{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{W}_{Q}\\mathbf{W}_{K}^{\\top}\\mathbf{H}^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_{h}}}\\right)\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O}.$$  |  | (2.1)\nL78: \nL79: The gradient of the value projection takes the form\nL80:  | $$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{V}}L=\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\\top},\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\mathbf{G}=\\nabla_{(\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O})}L.$$  |\nL81: Here, $\\mathbf{G}$ denotes the gradient with respect to the entire product $\\mathbf{W}_{V}\\mathbf{W}_{O}$. Thus, $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ acts as a spectral filter on the gradient received by $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$. Writing $\\mathbf{W}_{O}=\\sum_{i}\\sigma_{i}\\mathbf{u}_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\\top}$, we have $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{V}}L=\\sum_{i}\\sigma_{i}(\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{v}_{i})\\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\\top}$.\nL82: This makes the role of the spectrum explicit: each component $\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{v}_{i}$ enters the gradient with weight $\\sigma_{i}$. If $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ is scaled orthogonal, $\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{W}_{O}^{\\top}$ preserves the spectrum of $\\mathbf{G}$ up to a common scale. At the opposite extreme, if $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ has rank $r$, the update is restricted to contributions from $r$ spectral directions.\nL83: More generally, as the spectrum becomes more concentrated, directions associated with smaller singular values contribute proportionally less to the update.\nL84: In fact, this partner-dependent filtering is ubiquitous in Transformers: analogous coupling appears between the query–key pair $(\\mathbf{W}_{Q},\\mathbf{W}_{K})$ and the up- and down-projections of MLP and expert blocks.\nL85: To isolate this mechanism from the surrounding Transformer computation, consider the generic factorization $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}$, where $\\mathbf{G}=\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}}L(\\mathbf{W})$. The simple chain rule gives $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{X}}L=\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\top},\\nabla_{\\mathbf{Y}}L=\\mathbf{X}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G},$ so each factor directly filters the gradient received by its partner. Under the continuous-time exact-polar Muon dynamics (cite41†Ambrosio et al., 2005 ; cite42†Peyré, 2026 ),\nL86:  | $$\\dot{\\mathbf{X}}=-\\eta_{t}^{X}\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\top}),\\qquad\\dot{\\mathbf{Y}}=-\\eta_{t}^{Y}\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{X}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G}),$$  |\nL87: \nL88: where $\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{M})=\\mathbf{U}\\mathbf{V}^{\\top}$ for an SVD $\\mathbf{M}=\\mathbf{U}\\mathbf{\\Sigma}\\mathbf{V}^{\\top}$. The instantaneous loss decrease is therefore\nL89: \nL90:  | $$-\\dot{L}=\\eta_{t}^{X}\\left\\|\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{Y}^{\\top}\\right\\|_{*}+\\eta_{t}^{Y}\\left\\|\\mathbf{X}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G}\\right\\|_{*}.$$  |\nL91: Thus, the same filtered gradients above are the quantities that determine Muon’s instantaneous loss decrease. The local spectral effect can therefore translate into a local effect on optimization speed.\nL92: To empirically demonstrate this effect, we conduct a reciprocal $V$–$O$ intervention. Let $\\mathbf{G}_{O}=\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{O}}L$ denote the gradient with respect to $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$, and let $\\mathbf{U}_{O}=\\operatorname{Polar}(\\mathbf{G}_{O})$ denote the corresponding Muon update direction.\nL93: At a 10k-step checkpoint, we raise the smaller singular values of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$ toward its largest singular value while keeping its singular vectors and spectral norm fixed, and update only $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ with Muon.\nL94: As shown in cite43†Figure 1(b) , with the Muon update scale $\\|\\mathbf{U}_{O}\\|_{F}$ held fixed, increasing the effective rank of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$ increases $\\|\\mathbf{G}_{O}\\|_{*}$—the loss decrease under Muon flow—while decreasing the gradient–weight alignment $\\cos(\\mathbf{G}_{O},\\mathbf{W}_{O})$. These all indicate that the same-sized Muon update has a stronger effect as the effective rank of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$ increases.\nL95: Consistently, cite44†Figure 1(c) also shows that updating $\\mathbf{W}_{O}$ yields a larger held-out loss decrease at higher effective rank of $\\mathbf{W}_{V}$.\nL96: cite45†Image: Refer to caption (a) Absolute singular-value spectra.\nL97: \nL98: (b) Gradient and update metrics.\nL99: \nL100: (c) Held-out loss decrease.\nL101: \nL102: Figure 2.1: Single-layer $V$-spectrum intervention with $O$-only Muon updates. $\\mathbf{G}_{O}$ and $\\mathbf{U}_{O}$ denote the gradient and Muon update direction. In (c), rollout steps and loss decrease use logarithmic scales.\nL103: These observations suggest a simple principle: spectral concentration can create an optimization bottleneck. For a nonzero matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, define its effective rank by $\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{A}):=\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\right\\|_{F}^{2}/\\left\\|\\mathbf{A}\\right\\|_{2}^{2}$. To make this intuition concrete, we study a matrix-factorization model. The following bounds illustrate one possible mathematical pathway through which spectral structure may affect optimization.\nL104: ###### Theorem 2.1 (Low Effective Rank Limits Descent Speed).\nL105: For $k\\geq 2$, let $\\mathbf{X}$ and $\\mathbf{Y}$ be conformable matrices with $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times k}$, and consider the column-wise softmax loss $$L(\\mathbf{W})=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\left[\\log\\!\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{k}e^{\\mathbf{W}_{ji}}\\right)-\\mathbf{W}_{c_{i}i}\\right],\\quad c_{i}\\in[k],\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}$$ (2.2) let $(\\mathbf{X}_{t},\\mathbf{Y}_{t})_{t\\in[0,T]}$ be an absolutely continuous solution of the exact-polar Muon flow above, with $\\eta_{0}\\geq 0$ and $0\\leq\\eta_{t}^{X}\\leq\\eta_{0}/\\left\\|\\mathbf{Y}_{t}\\right\\|_{2},\\qquad 0\\leq\\eta_{t}^{Y}\\leq\\eta_{0}/\\left\\|\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\right\\|_{2}.$ Assume that the factor norms satisfy $\\left\\|\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\right\\|_{2},\\left\\|\\mathbf{Y}_{t}\\right\\|_{2}>0$ for all $t\\in[0,T]$.\nL106: Writing $L_{t}=L(\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\mathbf{Y}_{t})$, we have $$L_{T}\\geq L_{0}\\exp\\!\\left[-\\sqrt{2}\\,\\eta_{0}\\int_{0}^{T}\\left(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{X}_{s})}+\\sqrt{\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{Y}_{s})}\\right)ds\\right].$$\nL107: cite46†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2.2: Validation loss under persistent spectral constraints.\nL108: In this factorization model, the bound relates the rate of loss decrease to the effective ranks of the factors. Naturally, broad spectral support may alleviate this bottleneck, as demonstrated by the intervention experiments in cite47†Figure 2.1 . We therefore test whether maintaining such support throughout LLaMA pretraining helps by imposing progressively stronger spectral constraints. However, cite48†Figure 2.2 shows that persistent orthogonal constraints worsen validation loss.\nL109: Thus, the path-side advantage identified above does not translate into a benefit when the same structure is enforced persistently. This leads to our second question:\nL110: Q2. If broad spectral structure alleviates an optimization bottleneck, why enforcing it hurts performance?\nL111: The key is that a property that is favorable along the optimization trajectory need not be favorable when imposed as a persistent constraint. Spectral control does more than influence how an optimizer moves: when enforced throughout training, it also restricts the spectral structure of the solutions that the optimizer is allowed to reach. For example, an orthogonality constraint $\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}\\approx\\mathbf{I}$ forces the singular values of $\\mathbf{W}$ to remain nearly equal.\nL112: If the task prefers a more anisotropic or lower-effective-rank solution, such a constraint can prevent the optimizer from reaching it.\nL113: The factorized model above makes this competing effect concrete. For the same column-wise softmax objective in Eq. (cite49†2.2 ), the following theorem shows that sufficiently high effective rank can itself impose a nonzero loss floor.\nL114: ###### Theorem 2.2 (High Effective Rank Can Impose a Loss Floor).\nL115: Assume $k\\geq 2$, $\\mathbf{W}\\neq 0$, and $\\|\\mathbf{W}\\|_{2}\\leq B$ for a fixed scale bound $B=O(1)$. Let $K=\\left|\\{c_{i}:i\\in[k]\\}\\right|$ denote the number of distinct target indices. Then $$L(\\mathbf{W})\\geq C_{k}(\\mathbf{W})\\,k\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\frac{K}{\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{W})}}\\right),$$ where $C_{k}(\\mathbf{W})>0$ is the scale factor determined by $k$ and $\\|\\mathbf{W}\\|_{F}/\\sqrt{k}$.\nL116: The rank factor $1-\\sqrt{K/\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{W})}$ increases with effective rank and becomes positive when $\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{W})>K$.\nL117: Thus, for this objective, sufficiently high effective rank is not merely unnecessary at the optimum: it can be incompatible with arbitrarily small loss. In particular, if $\\mathbf{X}$ and $\\mathbf{Y}$ are $k\\times k$ scaled orthogonal matrices, then $\\operatorname{erank}(\\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y})=k$, and Theorem cite50†2.2 gives the positive endpoint loss floor $C_{k}(\\mathbf{W})\\,k\\left(1-\\sqrt{K/k}\\right)$ whenever $K<k$.\nL118: Together, these results illustrate a tension in the stylized model: broad spectral support can relax a path-side bottleneck, while a persistent spectral constraint can restrict low-loss solutions. We also test whether these intuitions are useful in the empirical training. The same spectral property can therefore be useful as a transient feature of the optimization trajectory while being harmful as a persistent constraint on the final solution.\nL119: This distinction helps reconcile the empirical behavior above. The high effective rank naturally maintained by Muon can support optimization without prescribing a fixed spectral geometry for the final weights. By contrast, increasingly restrictive spectral constraints preserve the desired structure by reducing the freedom of the model to adapt its spectrum as training proceeds.\nL120: The appropriate goal is therefore not to maximize effective rank at every point in training, but to retain enough spectral support to avoid path-side bottlenecks while still allowing the spectrum to adapt to the task.\nL121: ## 3 ORCA: A Minimal Shape-then-Release Optimizer\nL122: The preceding analysis reveals a tension between the trajectory-side benefit and the endpoint-side cost of spectral control. Broad spectral support can facilitate optimization along the training trajectory, while enforcing the same structure persistently may restrict task-adaptive solutions at the endpoint. This suggests a temporal design principle: spectral control should shape the early optimization trajectory, but be released before it restricts task adaptation.\nL123: ORCA (Orthogonal Regularization, Cooled After) is a minimal two-stage realization of this shape-then-release principle. During an initial active stage, ORCA applies spectral shaping to steer the early trajectory away from spectrally concentrated regimes. After this stage, the shaping term is removed completely and training reverts to standard Muon. This separation allows spectral control to influence the optimization path without prescribing the spectral geometry of the final solution.\nL124: ### 3.1 Algorithm\nL125: \nL126: A minimal realization: soft orthogonal shaping. For this minimal realization, ORCA uses soft orthogonality as its spectral shaping operator. Consider a matrix parameter $\\mathbf{W}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p\\times q}$, and assume $p\\leq q$ for notational simplicity. We define $\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W})=1/4\\ \\left\\|\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}-I_{p}\\right\\|_{F}^{2},$ whose gradient is\nL127:  | $$G_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W}):=\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}}\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W})=\\left(\\mathbf{W}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}-I_{p}\\right)W.$$  |  | (3.1)\nL128: \nL129: Writing a thin singular value decomposition as $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{U}\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_{1},\\ldots,\\sigma_{p})\\mathbf{V}^{\\top}$, we have\nL130:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W})=\\frac{1}{4}\\sum_{i=1}^{p}\\left(\\sigma_{i}^{2}-1\\right)^{2},\\quad\\mathbf{G}_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W})=\\mathbf{U}\\operatorname{diag}\\left((\\sigma_{i}^{2}-1)\\sigma_{i}\\right)_{i=1}^{p}\\mathbf{V}^{\\top}.$  |  | (3.2)\nL131: Thus, a gradient step on $\\mathcal{R}_{\\mathrm{orth}}$ decreases singular values above unit scale and increases nonzero singular values below unit scale, tending to reduce spectral concentration and increase the effective-rank measure considered in the preceding analysis. The operator requires neither an explicit SVD nor a projection onto the Stiefel manifold and can be computed using matrix multiplications alone.\nL132: ORCA update. Let $\\mathbf{U}_{t}$ denote the standard Muon update direction at step $t$, $\\eta_{t}$ the learning rate, $\\lambda$ the decoupled weight-decay coefficient, and $\\gamma_{t}$ the spectral-shaping strength. For each matrix parameter $\\mathbf{W}_{t}$, the ORCA update can be written as\nL133: \nL134:  | $$\\mathbf{W}_{t+1}=\\mathbf{W}_{t}-\\eta_{t}\\mathbf{U}_{t}-\\eta_{t}\\lambda\\mathbf{W}_{t}-\\eta_{t}\\gamma_{t}\\mathbf{G}_{\\mathrm{orth}}(\\mathbf{W}_{t}).$$  |  | (3.3)\nL135: The weight-decay and spectral-shaping corrections are both evaluated at the pre-update parameter $\\mathbf{W}_{t}$ (cite51†Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017 ), while $\\mathbf{U}_{t}$ is computed in the same way as in standard Muon.\nL136: \nL137: Similar to Muon and other matrix-based optimizers, ORCA is applied only to matrix parameters in the matrix-optimizer group. Embeddings, the language-model head, and one-dimensional parameters continue to follow their original AdamW updates.\nL138: Release rule. The defining feature of ORCA is that spectral shaping is transient rather than persistent. Let $T$ denote the total number of training steps and $\\tau\\in[0,1]$ the active fraction. The active window has length $S$, and we use the hard release schedule\nL139: \nL140:  | $$\\gamma_{t}=\\begin{cases}\\gamma,&t<S,\\\\\nL141: 0,&t\\geq S,\\end{cases}\\qquad S=\\left\\lfloor\\tau T\\right\\rfloor.$$  |  | (3.4)\nL142: Equation (cite52†3.4 ) makes the two phases explicit. During the active stage ($t<S$), ORCA augments Muon with the spectral-shaping term in Eq. (cite53†3.3 ). During the released stage ($t\\geq S$), the shaping term vanishes and the optimizer reverts exactly to the corresponding standard Muon training rule.\nL143: The hard switch is the simplest implementation of the shape-then-release principle. The early intervention is used to steer the optimization trajectory away from spectrally concentrated regimes, rather than to prescribe a persistent spectral constraint. Once released, the spectrum is free to adapt to the task objective.\nL144: Empirically, we find that part of the higher-effective-rank state induced during the active stage persists after release, allowing the optimization benefit to continue without maintaining the orthogonal regularizer. Unless otherwise specified, we use $\\tau=0.25$ throughout the paper; Section 5 studies sensitivity to the release point.\nL145: Beyond soft orthogonality. Soft orthogonality defines the default ORCA optimizer, but the underlying shape-then-release principle is not tied to this particular spectral operator. In Section 5, we replace soft orthogonal shaping with alternative early spectral interventions, including Pion-start and DiagSVD variants.\nL146: These methods differ in how the spectral state is shaped during the active stage; once the active window ends, the intervention is removed and all variants continue with the same released Muon dynamics. This provides a controlled way to study whether the benefit of early spectral intervention extends beyond soft orthogonality itself.\nL147: Implementation and overhead. In the standard setting, ORCA adds two matrix multiplications per weight matrix during shaping without changing the communication complexity; the remaining operations are fused into the optimizer update. Our Muon baseline performs 24 matrix multiplications across eight Newton–Schulz iterations, so shaping increases the multiplication count by $2/24=8.3\\%$. This extra work disappears after release.\nL148: We batch matrices of identical shapes to reduce kernel launches, use dedicated kernels, and form the Gram matrix along the shorter dimension. On the 8B MoE model, average distributed optimizer update time is $227.2\\,\\mathrm{ms}$ for Muon and $237.4\\,\\mathrm{ms}$ for ORCA. The $10.2\\,\\mathrm{ms}$ difference amounts to approximately $0.8\\%$ of a roughly $1.3\\,\\mathrm{s}$ training step. Peak memory is $101.41\\,\\mathrm{GB}$ and $101.66\\,\\mathrm{GB}$, respectively.\nL149: Overall, ORCA incurs negligible additional training overhead in this configuration.\nL150: ## 4 Experiment Validations\nL151: \nL152: In this section, we verify the efficacy of ORCA on three specific issues: (1) Llama pretraining with grid learning rate search and larger scale pretraining. (2) Pretraining task on Qwen3 architecture with the additional QK-norm that may violate the matrix factoring reparameterization. This section focuses on the efficacy of ORCA, and for more robustness test of parameters and controlled interventions on gain of ORCA, readers can refer to Section cite12†5 .\nL153: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup and Tuning Protocol\nL154: Setups. We pre-train Llama-series models cite54†Touvron et al. (2023) ranging from 130M to 1.3B parameters on C4 , using 25B to 100B tokens. We also pre-train Qwen3-series models cite55†Yang et al. (2025) ranging from 160M to 0.6B parameters on The Pile (cite56†Gao et al., 2020 ), using 25B to 100B tokens.\nL155: Finally, we train fine-grained mixture-of-experts models ranging from 0.8B to 8B parameters on OLMo-mix-1124 (cite57†OLMo et al., 2024 ), the data mixture including DCLM-Baseline (cite58†Li et al., 2024 ) using 25B to 100B tokens.\nL156: We first run a learning rate grid-search for Adam, Muon, Pion and ORCA. All trials share the same data, batch size, sequence length, and 50k-step training budget. We use $\\mathbf{O}_{t}=0.2/\\mathrm{Rms}(\\mathbf{O}_{t})\\cdot\\mathbf{O}_{t}$ to rescale the Muon and ORCA update where $\\mathbf{O}_{t}$ is the Newton-Schulz orthogonalization output to align the update with the Adam’s (cite33†Liu et al., 2025 ). We perform a two-stage coarse-to-fine learning rate grid search.\nL157: The coarse sweep evaluates $\\{5\\times 10^{-4},10^{-3},2\\times 10^{-3},5\\times 10^{-3}\\}$. Once the coarse sweep locates a candidate learning rate, we evaluate the neighbors on its both sides to refine the best lr within a smaller local range. The refinement grid consists of $\\{6,8\\}\\times 10^{-4}$, $\\{1.3,1.6\\}\\times 10^{-3}$, and $\\{3,4\\}\\times 10^{-3}$, which reduces the local spacing between learning rates candidates to about $1.2\\times$–$1.5\\times$.\nL158: All four optimizers use this protocol and the same tuning budget.\nL159: The advantage of ORCA holds over a wide range of learning rates: at every learning rate evaluated for both methods, ORCA reaches a lower final validation loss than Muon, and at all evaluated configurations between $10^{-3}$ and $5\\times 10^{-3}$ it is even better than fully tuned Muon.\nL160: The independent searches select $1.3\\times 10^{-3}$, $3\\times 10^{-3}$, $10^{-3}$, and $2\\times 10^{-3}$ for Adam, Muon, Pion, and ORCA, with final validation losses of $2.8824$, $2.8460$, $2.9790$, and $2.8106$, respectively; ORCA thus lowers the loss by a further $0.0354$ relative to the best Muon and stays within $0.002$ of its own optimum across $1.6\\times 10^{-3}$ to $3\\times 10^{-3}$, showing that the improvement does not rely on a narrow learning-rate choice.\nL161: LLama Pretraining Tasks. We compare ORCA against Adam and Muon on LLaMA 350M and 1.3B, training each model for 100B tokens. As shown in cite59†Figure 4.1 , Muon outperforms Adam at both scales, while ORCA further lowers the validation loss. Compared with Muon, ORCA reduces the final loss by $0.0194$ at 350M and $0.0244$ at 1.3B, with consistent gains at both scales. Further, a four-seed robustness result for the 130M, 50k-step setting is reported in Appendix cite21†C .\nL162: We also evaluate the final ORCA and Muon LLaMA 1.3B checkpoints from cite59†Figure 4.1 on five downstream tasks (cite60†Clark et al., 2018 ; cite61†Zellers et al., 2019 ; cite62†Sakaguchi et al., 2021 ; cite63†Bisk et al., 2020 ). As shown in cite64†Table 4.1 , ORCA outperforms Muon on all five tasks. This suggests that the lower pre-training loss of ORCA also leads to better downstream performance.\nL163: We also evaluate how the optimization performance translates into token efficiency by measuring the training required to reach a common target loss. We continued ORCA from its original 51k-step checkpoint, starting the final decay early, and reached a validation loss of 2.318 at approximately 60k steps. The standard Muon run, using its original 100k-step settings, reached the same loss at 100k steps (cite65†Figure 2(a) ).\nL164: Therefore, ORCA used 40% fewer tokens to reach this target, a 1.66× token-efficiency gain. To save compute, we reused the checkpoint without retuning ORCA for the shorter run: its original 25k-step shaping stage accounts for 41.7% of the 60k-step budget rather than the tuned default of 25%.\nL165: cite66†Image: Refer to caption (a) Learning-rate sweep\nL166: \nL167: cite67†Image: Refer to caption (b) LLaMA 350M\nL168: \nL169: cite68†Image: Refer to caption (c) LLaMA 1.3B\nL170: \nL171: Figure 4.1: LLaMA pretraining results.\nL172: \nL173: cite69†Image: Refer to caption (a) Matched-target token efficiency\nL174: \nL175: (b) Shaping strength $\\gamma$\nL176: \nL177: (c) Active-window fraction $\\tau$\nL178: Figure 4.2: Token efficiency and shaping ablations in LLaMA Pretraining. Table 4.1: Downstream evaluation on LLaMA 1.3B.\nL179: Optimizer  | ARC-C  | ARC-E  | HellaSwag  | PIQA  | WinoGrande  | Avg.\nL180: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL181: Muon  | $0.305\\,(0.013)$  | $0.342\\,(0.010)$  | $0.549\\,(0.005)$  | $0.705\\,(0.011)$  | $0.547\\,(0.014)$  | $0.489$\nL182: ORCA  | $\\mathbf{0.311}\\,(0.014)$  | $\\mathbf{0.357}\\,(0.010)$  | $\\mathbf{0.573}\\,(0.005)$  | $\\mathbf{0.711}\\,(0.011)$  | $\\mathbf{0.553}\\,(0.014)$  | $\\mathbf{0.501}$\nL183: Qwen3 Pretraining Tasks. We compare Adam, Muon, and ORCA on Qwen3-style architecture. Within each model scale, all methods share the same common hyperparameters. The Qwen3 0.6B curves cover 50k optimizer steps (about 105B tokens), and the Qwen3 160M curves cover 25k optimizer steps (about 26B tokens). In both Qwen settings, both sets of loss curves show the same ordering: ORCA may fall temporarily behind Muon early in training, then crosses over and holds the advantage at the end of the observed interval.\nL184: The validation-loss improvement of ORCA is therefore not confined to LLaMA, but also reproduces on the Qwen3 architecture.\nL185: Larger Scale Fine-grained MoE Pretrining. We observed router perturbations under a fixed global release point, so we release the orthogonal constraint layerwise: at $20\\%$ of training in the first layer and at $60\\%$ in the last layer, with release times linearly interpolated across layers. We use top-$4$ routing with $64$ experts in the MoE $0.8$B model ($0.24$B active parameters) and $64$ experts in the MoE $8$B model ($1$B active parameters) (cite70†Dai et al., 2024 ).\nL186: Due to computational budget constraints, we focus our comparison on Muon and ORCA. ORCA achieves the lowest final loss at both $25$B and $50$B tokens for the $0.8$B model, showing that its gain persists as the token budget increases. The $8$B experiment extends this result to a larger model trained on $100$B tokens, where ORCA also finishes below Muon (cite71†Figure 4.4 ). Across these MoE experiments, ORCA reduces final validation loss by $0.020$–$0.025$ relative to Muon.\nL187: Early orthogonal shaping also benefits other base optimizers. We apply the same soft orthogonal shaping step (cite53†3.3 ) to Adam and SOAP on LLaMA 130M (cite72†Vyas et al., 2025 ). Shaping is active only during the first $25\\%$ of training; the base optimizer then continues alone. For both optimizers, shaping temporarily raises validation loss while active, but the shaped runs overtake their baselines after its removal and finish lower.\nL188: At 50k steps, it reduces Adam’s loss from $2.8824$ to $2.8435$ and SOAP’s from $2.8640$ to $2.8271$, gains of $0.0389$ and $0.0369$, respectively. These results show that the benefits of early orthogonal shaping extend beyond Muon to other base optimizers.\nL189: (a) Qwen3 160M\nL190: \nL191: (b) Qwen3 0.6B\nL192: \nL193: (c) LLaMA 130M, base optimizers\nL194: \nL195: Figure 4.3: Validation loss across architectures and base optimizers.\nL196: \nL197: cite73†Image: Refer to caption (a) MoE $0.8$B, $25$B tokens\nL198: \nL199: cite74†Image: Refer to caption (b) MoE $0.8$B, $50$B tokens\nL200: \nL201: cite75†Image: Refer to caption (c) MoE $8$B $1$B active, $100$B tokens\nL202: \nL203: Figure 4.4: Fine-grained MoE validation loss with layer-wise release.\nL204: ## 5 Where the Gain Comes From: Controlled Interventions\nL205: cite40†Figure 1.1 offers an initial view of ORCA’s mechanism: early shaping raises the effective rank, part of this increase persists after shaping is released, and loss then decreases faster. While cite10†Section 4 establishes ORCA’s optimization gains, in this section we vary shaping strength and timing, as well as the form of the early spectral intervention, to isolate their contributions to these gains.\nL206: Additional controls for optimizer state and learning-rate schedules are reported separately in Appendix cite23†C.2 .\nL207: Retained Effective Rank Predicts Post-Release Gain We first test whether the effective-rank difference retained after release tracks ORCA’s final gain. Holding all other settings fixed, we sweep the shaping strength over $\\gamma\\in\\{0,0.05,0.1,0.5,1,2\\}$ and measure both the average post-release effective rank and the final validation loss. The sweep exhibits a clear dose response.\nL208: As $\\gamma$ increases, the retained effective rank rises from about $278$ to $335$, while the final validation loss falls from $2.8466$ to $2.8106$ (cite76†Figure 2(b) ). The relation flattens at larger $\\gamma$, indicating diminishing returns. This supports retained effective rank as an important variable behind ORCA’s post-release gain.\nL209: Release-point sensitivity. We first vary the duration of the initial shaping stage over $\\tau\\in\\{0.10,0.25,0.40,0.55\\}$ on LLaMA 130M. Every setting improves over Muon’s final loss of $2.8512$: the four release points reach $2.8210$, $2.8106$, $2.8107$, and $2.8166$, respectively (cite77†Figure 2(c) ). The broad effective range shows that ORCA does not rely on a precise release point. We use $\\tau=0.25$ by default because it is the shortest tested window in the best-performing range.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.05265v1] Loopy: Low-Bit Quantization Framework for Looped Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05265v1)\nciteturn41353view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05265v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.06116v1] ORCA: The Annealed Spectral Conditioning Optimizer for Faster, Better LLM Training (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06116v1)\nciteturn41353view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06116v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\n"
